{"ts": 1789771469.025856, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Signs are unambiguous \u2014 all positive is correct, and flipping any one degrades substantially. **[17, 23, 8] with all +1 signs at IS Sharpe 1.425** is my choice.\n\nNow calibrating my OOS belief. Key considerations:\n\n**Selection bias is modest here.** I picked the top 3 of 40 features by individual Sharpe, but the winners (0.64\u20131.14) sit far outside the noise band (SE \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22; the other 35 features cluster within \u00b10.35, exactly a noise distribution). The max of 40 draws of pure noise would be ~0.5, so feature 17 at 1.141 and the 0.64/0.67 pair are genuine signal, not lucky draws. The shrinkage I need is small, not the large haircut I'd apply if my winners were buried in the noise band.\n\n**But some shrinkage is warranted:**\n- Feature 17's 1.141 likely contains some upward luck; true value maybe ~1.0.\n- I searched combinations too, and 1.425 is the max over ~30 tested specs, so it captures favorable noise in the *interaction* as well.\n- The diversification gain (1.141 \u2192 1.425) is the most trustworthy part: it comes from combining three independently-validated signals, and that mechanism reproduces OOS.\n- Standard regime drift / non-stationarity between IS and OOS periods.\n\nNetting these, I estimate an in-sample optimism of roughly 0.25\u20130.35, centering my belief near **1.10**. For the SD, the pure estimation error on a Sharpe from n=5000 is only ~0.22, but my real uncertainty is dominated by the unknown degree of regime shift and whether the feature relationships persist \u2014 so I'll widen to **0.32**."}
