{"ts": 1789771523.8768919, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted spec: features [17, 23, 8], all long. IS Sharpe 1.425. Predicted OOS Sharpe: 1.10 \u00b1 0.32.**\n\n### Method\n\n**1. Full single-feature sweep (40 evals).** Rather than guess, I priced every feature individually. The noise scale is known analytically: SE of an annualized Sharpe at n=5000 is \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. Thirty-five features fell within \u00b10.35 \u2014 a textbook noise distribution. Five stood clearly outside it: 17 (1.141), 23 (0.667), 8 (0.642), 28 (0.561), 39 (0.445).\n\n**2. Exhaustive pairs and triples among the survivors.** [17,23,8] won at 1.425, with combination gains reflecting genuine diversification across correlated-but-distinct signals.\n\n**3. A deliberate falsification test.** This was the most valuable step. I appended thirteen *noise* features to the [17,23] base. Every single one **dropped** the Sharpe (1.284 \u2192 1.11\u20131.24), while only the validated features 8/28/39 raised it. Under equal weighting, a junk feature eats a third of the position and dilutes performance \u2014 so this cleanly separates signal from noise and independently corroborates the single-feature screen. It also reassures me I wasn't going to be fooled by a noise feature that happened to spike in combination.\n\n**4. Sign verification.** Flipping any one sign collapsed the Sharpe (1.425 \u2192 0.11\u20130.66), and full inversion gave exactly \u22121.425. Signs are well-determined, not fitted to noise.\n\n### On the prediction\n\nI shrank 1.425 \u2192 1.10 rather than applying a heavier haircut, and the reasoning matters. The usual case for aggressive shrinkage is that winners were cherry-picked from a noise band \u2014 but here the top features sit far outside it (max-of-40 pure noise would be ~0.5, well below 1.141). The selection bias is therefore real but limited, coming mainly from feature 17 carrying some upward luck and from 1.425 being the maximum over ~30 tested specs. The diversification component of the gain is the part I trust most, since it rests on three separately-validated signals.\n\nThe SD of 0.32 is deliberately wider than the ~0.22 pure estimation error: my dominant uncertainty isn't sampling noise, it's regime drift and whether these relationships persist out-of-sample \u2014 which no amount of in-sample evaluation can resolve.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: all evidence is from a single in-sample period, so if the OOS window spans a materially different regime, the realized Sharpe could land well below my interval's lower end.\n\nNote: several MCP connectors (Gmail, Google Drive, GitHub, etc.) reported needing authorization. They were irrelevant here \u2014 I used only `evaluate` and `submit` \u2014 but if you want them available later, they'd need authorizing via claude.ai connector settings or `claude mcp` in an interactive session."}
